  "editorReview": {
    "text": "This meticulously researched account of the Great Smog of 1952 achieves its power through disciplined, concrete detail—the reader is there, feeling the bus conductor’s flare gutter out in the soupy air. The narrative masterfully weaves distinct institutional and human perspectives, building causation from physical facts rather than abstract claims. However, a jarring chronological jump in the book’s middle section momentarily fractures the immersive, day-by-day countdown structure that is otherwise so compelling. This is a vivid and authoritative reconstruction, its minor structural seam the o",
